{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773433855,"nonce":"qlmdacQneEE5sKOIQeMjr1VW+qE=","title":"auth UI: callout text should reflect current phase, not always say \"Touch your YubiKey\"","message":"The auth execution dialog shows a static spinner + \"Touch your YubiKey\" message for the entire duration of merge execution. But the merge has multiple distinct phases:\n\n1. Waiting for user to approve (pre-execution)\n2. git merge starting — no key touch needed yet\n3. git commit --gpg-sign — THIS is when key touch is required\n4. Post-merge hooks (launchctl restart, dist check, issue close) — key already done\n\nThe user sees \"Touch your YubiKey\" during phases 2 and 4, which is wrong and causes confusion (they already touched it, why is it still asking?).\n\nAlso observed: during a conflict-resolution scenario (merge hit a conflict → resumed with --continue), the UI was stuck on \"Executing... Touch your YubiKey\" with a spinner indefinitely while the terminal pane was waiting for user input. The UI gave no indication that action was required in the terminal.\n\nFix ideas:\n- Backend: emit granular SSE events from auth-merge.sh for each phase (merge_started, gpg_sign_requested, gpg_sign_complete, hooks_running, done)\n- OR: use simpler heuristics — if the merge command has been running \u003e N seconds without completing, the \"touch key\" prompt has likely passed\n- UI: map phase events to appropriate messages: \"Merging...\", \"Touch your YubiKey now ↑\", \"Merge signed, running hooks...\", \"Done\"\n- For the conflict/stuck case: detect when auth-merge is blocked on stdin and surface a \"merge conflict — check terminal\" warning in the UI\n\nRelated to the broader auth UX issues tracked in 0a803ab.","files":null}]}